A||||13||batboy||which temp sensor is which?
Z||000000||batboy||07-17-99||10:44 PM||||I've found what appears to be five wires running to three temperature sensors (in the thermostat housing and intake manifold). I think the sending unit with one green/white wire is for the cooling fan. One of the others must be the gauge sending unit, but which one is it? What is the other one for? I know there is a temp indicator light in the dash, is this what it's for or is it a sensor for the computer (which I'm not using)? I'm so confused.
Z||000001||Phil||07-18-99||04:59 AM||||The one for the gage/light is in the block as is the fan switch,#3 is for the computer
Z||000002||batboy||07-18-99||06:41 AM||||Ok, that makes sense. But, what color are the two wires that run to the gauge sending unit?
Z||000003||batboy||07-18-99||02:11 PM||||I've determined that the temperature warning light in the dash does not light up when you first turn on the ignition. I believe the gauge and the light operate off the same sending unit. I'm going to try another sending unit and look one more time for a bad ground. If still no joy, I'm going to run some new wires. Does the original temp gauge wires run down the console, does anybody know?
Z||000004||Phil||07-18-99||05:01 PM||||Just ground both wires -gage should peg and light should go on(ign. on of course)
Z||000005||batboy||07-18-99||08:12 PM||||Ok Phil, I found the right sending unit. When I ground one green wire the temp gauge pegs and when I ground the other green wire the temp light comes on. So that means the wires must be good, the light is ok, and the gauge appears to be working. By a process of elimination, it just about has to be a bad sending unit, right? I'll pick one up tomorrow and try it. Never knew about that grounding the wires trick, thanks Phil!
Z||000006||theogre||07-19-99||07:35 AM||||There is a known wiring error in the temp gauge/light circuits. There is an article here some place with a link to good instructions for correcting the error.
Z||000007||Phil||07-19-99||03:25 PM||||Ogre --  Is that the one about stopping the temp gage from pegging on start up?
Z||000008||GREENDANO||07-19-99||04:03 PM||||A friend of mine might have the instructions you need to rapair the pegging gauge.  It rewires the circuit so the temp light lights durring cranking/bulb check and the gauge no longer pegs.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000009||batboy||07-19-99||08:02 PM||||I put a new temp gauge sending unit in and I now have a working gauge. Happy, happy, joy, joy. Although, I still can't get the temp light to come on during crank up. It comes on if I ground the wire. The terminal plug seems a bit ratty and loose fitting, maybe the next time I'm at the boneyard I'll pick up another one. However for the time being, I'm just glad I got the gauge working. One more thing, the cooling fan kicks in at about 160 degrees, so that sensor/switch must be an aftermarket unit, which is fine with me I guess. Thanks for all your input guys.
Z||000010||ezsurf||07-20-99||07:59 PM||||batboy,<br>From your comments, I take it that you have the 4 cyl engine.  I have the V-6 in my car.  I also have a problem with the temp guage.  I am trying to determine if the problem is with the guage or the sending unit.  I ran the grounding test that Phil mentioned, the guage pegged and I got the light.  When I turn the ignition on the temp guage reads 175 deg when the engine is cold.  When the engine is hot it climbs to the base of the red 245 deg if I'm reading it correctly.  The guage tick marks represent 15 deg/mark below 220 deg, and 5 deg/mark above 220 deg. I don't believe the engine is overheating.  The guage is working, but it is not accurate.  Ogre, is this part of the wiring error you are talking about, or are you refering to something else?  Anyone have any ideas/tests/fixes? 
Z||000011||batboy||07-20-99||09:09 PM||||Ezsurf, I do have a 4 cylinder Fiero too, but actually the one I was referring to in this discussion was my V8 batmobile Fiero (which was formerly a V6 car). Having two Fieros, I've been swapping known good gauges and parts back and forth. In my case, the needle just always stayed right at 100 degrees (even with both gauges I tried). I didn't want to tear into my dash after the instrument cluster at first, but after doing it about a half dozen times now, it seems pretty easy. Find a good cheap gauge cluster (or just the temp/fuel gauge part of it) and swap it in. Sounds like your temp gauge is messed up. I've also heard of people removing the gauge needle and repositioning it (you may have to glue it back on). That may not be perfectly accurate, but would have to be much better than what you got now. 
Z||000012||ezsurf||07-20-99||09:37 PM||||batboy,<br>Does anyone make a reproduction guage, or do I have to rip one out of a junk car?  
Z||000013||batboy||07-20-99||10:22 PM||||I personally don't know of any reproduction gauges, but I sure don't know everything. I'd just find a good used one. Call Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory, I bet he has one he'd send you.
